# An early tester of Calc-U 59 by accident mistyped a program shown in TI PPC NOTES v6n6-7,1981
# The program is in article "FAST MODE" by Bob Arendt.
#
# There are supposed to be five zeroes at the start. When only four (or even less) are used, the
# calculator misbehaves in interesting ways.
#
# I want to preserve this interesting misbehave for future debug runs. Also, the program uncovered
# an issue with the emulation that has been fixed before v1.0.0.
#
# Load the program, then press R/S, then LRN: The display shows "5281 09". The same is seen on a
# real TI-59.
# After pressing R/S again, a blinking zero appears. However, the emulator initially had a bug:
# the display would flicker and briefly show "0.000 0" during this state.
#
# The root cause was that the emulator kept updating the display after the calculator entered
# IDLE (idle/waiting state), overwriting the intentional afterglow effect.
#
# Why the display updates after leaving IDLE? The application refreshes at 60 Hz, but the physical TI-59
# LEDs have a natural "afterglow"—they don't instantly vanish when turned off. To simulate this
# without introducing aliasing artifacts, the emulation deliberately keeps the
# display visible for several frames after the value stops changing. However, once the afterglow
# phase begins, the display must fade naturally without being overwritten by new values.
#
# Note that this does not work on the TI-58C: This calculator uses a different ROM, and does not
# fall into the same traps.

PROGRAM:
000  00  0
001  00  0
002  00  0
003  00  0
004  36 PGM
005  02  02
006  71 SBR
007  02  02
008  39  39
009  09  9
010  00  0
011  66 PAU
012  91 R/S
013  66 PAU
014  91 R/S
015  02  2
016  39 COS
017  09  9
018  00  0
019  66 PAU
020  91 R/S
021  66 PAU
022  81 RST
023  69 OP
024  00  00
025  01  1
026  06  6
027  03  3
028  02  2
029  03  3
030  01  1
031  01  1
032  07  7
033  00  0
034  00  0
035  69 OP
036  01  01
037  69 OP
038  03  03
039  69 OP
040  05  05
041  66 PAU
042  91 R/S